  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Initiating Backend Foundation**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the backend foundation. The focus is definitely on building out the Rust (Tauri) backend, ensuring a solid base before moving forward. The goal is clear: get that backend robust and working, with SQLite integration via `rusqlite` and `rusqlite_migration`, and also prepare TanStack Query.\n\n\n**Focusing on Settings & Data**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the Settings screen implementation. I\'m deep diving into fetching and mutating data. I need to get that Settings screen fully functional with the SQLite integration. Also, I\'m working with TanStack Query and managing interactions with the SQLite settings table using `rusqlite`.\n\n\n**Defining Mocking Strategy**\n\nI\'m now refining the mocking strategy to improve the delivery\'s user experience. It involves adapting API calls for the browser (AI Studio) and Tauri `invoke` for desktop, establishing a clear boundary. The file structure is being prepped, along with the migration and Tauri command implementations. I\'m focusing on providing instructions for local execution, explaining the mock/Tauri separation, outlining new dependencies, current limitations, and manual tests.\n\n\n**Establishing API Interaction**\n\nI\'m now establishing the API interaction boundary. My plan is to create `src/lib/tauri.ts` to manage the Tauri check and determine if I need to use `@tauri-apps/api/invoke` and `@tauri-apps/plugin-dialog`, or fall back to mock `localStorage` for the AI Studio preview. I\'ll also create `src/services/settingsApi.ts` and `src/queries/useSettings.ts`. UI integration in `src/pages/Settings.tsx` is next. I am now proceeding with folder selection button integration and the Rust code generation.\n\n\n**Constructing Initial Dependencies**\n\nI\'m now drafting the files with a focus on core dependencies. I\'m installing `@tanstack/react-query`, `@tauri-apps/api`, and `@tauri-apps/plugin-dialog`. The Rust files (`Cargo.toml`) are being defined, even though I can\'t compile here, but it\'s important to set the needed dependencies like tauri, rusqlite, and others. The React side includes `apiClient.ts` for Tauri invocation and dialog interactions.\n\n\n**Implementing Mock Interaction**\n\nI\'m now incorporating mock interactions for settings within the API boundary, so I can keep developing. I am simulating Tauri\'s `invoke` for local development. I have established a mock `get_settings` and `save_setting` implementation utilizing a simple `mockSettings` object, as well as an `open_dialog_folder` mock response. I\'m focusing on ensuring data retrieval and mutation function seamlessly in the absence of a live backend.\n\n\n**Refining DB Migration**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the database migrations. I\'m adding the migration logic to create the `settings` table in `src-tauri/src/database/migrations.rs`, using `rusqlite_migration`. I\'m using a `lazy_static!` block to ensure this is set up correctly. Also, I\'m working on setting up the `QueryClientProvider` within either `src/App.tsx` or `src/main.tsx` for TanStack Query integration. I\'m aiming for seamless data fetching and mutation as the backend solidifies.\n\n\n**Orchestrating Library Installation**\n\nI\'m currently automating the package installation, aiming for `@tanstack/react-query`, `@tauri-apps/api`, `@tauri-apps/plugin-dialog`, and `react-hot-toast` to be installed. I\'ll then create the necessary service, query, and settings components, as well as the main entry points to bring everything together. My plan includes checking the `main.tsx` for providers. Furthermore, I am ensuring the setup adheres to the established constraints, and am about to address the Rust backend.\n\n\n**Generating Project Structure**\n\nI\'m now generating the necessary project structure, focusing on the Tauri files that need to be created. These include `tauri.conf.json`, `Cargo.toml`, `main.rs`, and command/service/repository files. I\'m also preparing models and database components to ensure the user can clone and execute without needing `cargo` in the AI Studio container. I\'ve realized compilation isn\'t needed here.\n\n\n"
