  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining the Scope**\n\nI\'m currently focused on defining the scope for refining the backend (Rust/Tauri) and frontend (React) aspects of Step 2, ensuring no new features are included, particularly \"Empresas.\" The goal is to set a solid foundation before moving to Step 3. This is the top priority for now.\n\n\n**Refining the Storage**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing moving the SQLite database to the OS AppData directory with Tauri\'s path resolution. I also need to verify the dialog plugin configuration. The \"Save Settings\" button is crucial; I\'ll track unsaved changes to handle local state updates for folder selection and consolidate multiple `save_setting` calls into a single transaction managed by the Rust backend. I will also ensure mock separation so that there are never any fallback options on IPC errors. Finally, a clear list of deliverables is needed.\n\n\n**Isolating and Testing**\n\nI\'m now focusing on mock isolation and creating a checklist for Windows testing, which are high priority for Step 2. I have to update `Cargo.toml` for `tauri-plugin-dialog` and `tauri-plugin-fs` if needed and adjust `main.rs` to initialize the database inside the Tauri setup hook, because we need the `AppHandle` to resolve paths like `app_data_dir`. Finally, I need to update `tauri.conf.json` / capabilities if needed.\n\n\n**Implementing Transactions**\n\nI\'m currently focused on implementing a transaction for saving settings using `rusqlite`. This involves updating `commands/settings_commands.rs` to receive a `HashMap`, updating `services/settings_service.rs` to iterate and use a transaction, and updating the repository to support upserting within the transaction. I am also making sure that if `isTauri()` is true, the IPC call is always returned in `src/services/apiClient.ts` without a `catch` or fallback.\n\n\n**Updating the Hooks**\n\nI\'m now integrating `react-query` to detect unsaved changes and updating `useSettings.ts` to expose `useSaveSettings`, a mutation that takes a full settings object. I\'m also addressing Tauri v2-specific changes, ensuring path resolution using `app.path().app_data_dir()`, initializing `DbState` in `setup` for state management, and verifying the dialog plugin integration.\n\n\n**Configuring Access**\n\nI\'m now focusing on ensuring dialog access by providing a `capabilities/default.json` file. This should allow for dialog access. I\'ve also drafted some database code modifications. I\'m updating `src-tauri/src/database/connection.rs` with `DbState` and `establish_connection`. The `src-tauri/src/main.rs` file imports the modules and sets up the database connection.\n\n\n**Connecting the Database**\n\nI am now focusing on setting up the database connection within the Tauri application. I\'m leveraging `app.path().app_data_dir()` to locate the application data directory and establishing a connection using `establish_connection`. The `DbState` is managed and mutexed and integrated into the Tauri setup. I am setting the database path as a join of the app data directory and `nisti_work.db`.\n\n\n**Implementing Upserts**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `upsert_many` function for saving multiple settings using a transaction in `src-tauri/src/services/settings_service.rs` and `src-tauri/src/repositories/settings_repository.rs`. The repository will use a prepared statement to upsert the data within a single transaction to ensure atomicity.\n\n\n**Refactoring for Transactions**\n\nI\'m now implementing the refactoring for transactions. I\'ve updated the database connection handling in `settings_commands.rs` to pass a mutable reference to the `Connection`. In addition, I have removed the fallback mechanism in `apiClient.ts` to ensure that IPC calls in Tauri always return the Tauri result, throwing the error directly. I\'ve also updated the `Settings` UI to use the batch `save_settings` command, and I will track changes with deep comparison.\n\n\n**Verifying Capability Access**\n\nI\'m now making sure that the Tauri v2 dialog plugin is correctly permissioned. I\'ll focus on creating the `default.json` in the `/src-tauri/capabilities/` directory, following the correct schema and permissions, or if I can, modify `tauri.conf.json`. If capabilities folder are automatically loaded, I will make sure it loads. I will skip migrating for now.\n\n\n**Adjusting Configuration**\n\nI\'m now focused on transitioning to Tauri v2\'s permissioning. I\'ll remove the `allowlist` from `tauri.conf.json`, as it\'s a Tauri v1 concept, and create a `capabilities/default.json` file. I am also reviewing which other files I need to modify for these changes, including the main entry point, database connection and setting commands.\n\n\n**Implementing Save Logic**\n\nI\'m now implementing the explicit save logic and state tracking in `/src/pages/Settings.tsx`, and refining the UI accordingly. I\'m also ensuring the `tauri.conf.json` is v2 compatible to avoid errors, while focusing on minimal modifications. I\'m creating the default capabilities.json file. I will make sure main.rs has the necessary database setup.\n\n\n**Simplifying Handler Exports**\n\nI\'m now streamlining the Tauri command handling. I\'ve removed the individual `save_setting` function export from `main.rs` and the handler generation. This is to ensure I\'m using the batch `save_settings` and keep code cleaner. I\'m focusing on ensuring it uses the combined handler. I will remove the single call and now only use the batch call in settings UI.\n\n\n**Refining Local State**\n\nI\'m now focusing on refining the local state management for settings in the React component. I am using `useState` and `useEffect` to ensure the component is always in sync with the settings. I\'m leveraging `react-query`\'s `useSettings` hook to fetch data. I\'m also preparing the use of the `isDirty` state with a deep comparison to ensure a save action is always triggered.\n\n\n**Validating Folder Selection**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the folder selection logic. I\'m ensuring that when a folder is selected, it correctly updates the local settings state using `setLocalSettings`. Also, I will use `saveSettingsMutation.mutate` to persist changes. I\'ll need to handle the loading state properly to provide good user feedback.\n\n\n**Finalizing State Handling**\n\nI\'m now putting the finishing touches on the settings state management. I\'ve integrated the `isDirty` state with a deep comparison to accurately track changes and trigger the save action. The `saveSettingsMutation.mutate` function is ready, and I\'ve ensured a smooth loading state experience for the user. I\'m also ensuring the settings UI uses `localSettings` and is synchronized at all times.\n\n\n**Executing the Edits**\n\nI\'m now in the process of systematically implementing the changes. I\'m focused on creating the `default.json` in the capabilities directory and making edits to the `main.rs` file to ensure the database connection is correctly established. I\'m also carefully modifying the database, commands, services, and repository files. I am paying close attention to the `apiClient.ts`, `useSettings.ts` and `Settings.tsx` files to ensure all interactions are smooth. I\'m double-checking `tauri.conf.json` for any necessary adjustments related to permissions.\n\n\n"
